UNIT carried_declared
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@6199e2cc8b75 cli/sir/v05.mjs carriedDeclared(primaryText) over cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs parse (legacy mode) — the
  primary's CARRIED declaration read through the parser; reproduce it exactly, accidents included
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG carried_declared(text: text) -> record[name: text, from: text, sha: text | none, shape: text | none, assertions: list[record[expr: text, equals: json]]] | none
ENVELOPE text is well-formed (no lone surrogate). Inside an AUTHORITY value (R10) no escape denotes a lone surrogate, and json
  nests at most 64 deep. Throws are IN scope as observations: R6 R8 R9 R10 R11 say when.
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Read the carried-table declaration out of the text of a SIR primary, through the SIR line parser: the one CARRIED
  directive's head gives the table's name and source; its own continuation lines give the CANONICAL-SHA256 hash, the SHAPE and
  the AUTHORITY { expr, equals } assertions, each equals parsed as JSON. No CARRIED directive -> none. A malformed head, hash,
  SHAPE or AUTHORITY line, a sub-field keyword that lost its indent, a repeated hash or SHAPE, or a second CARRIED throws; nothing partial is returned. The record's
  fields, in order: name, from, sha, shape, assertions; each assertion is (expr, equals). All positions are code points.
BEHAVIOR
R1 Lines. SP = space or tab, the only separator: every other character (no-break space, U+3000, U+2028 …) is content. The
  text splits into lines at CRLF, CR and LF only. In each line, every U+FEFF inside the line's leading run of SP and U+FEFF
  (in any mix) is removed, then trailing SP is removed; the line's INDENT is then its leading SP run, and its CONTENT the rest.
  A line that is now empty is skipped.
R2 Comments. HASH is the number sign U+0023. A line whose CONTENT starts with HASH is a comment line: skipped entirely (it
  neither starts nor ends a directive). Otherwise the CODE is the CONTENT cut before its first HASH that is preceded by SP,
  followed by SP or the end, and not inside a quoted span (PINNED grammar QUOTED-SPAN), with trailing SP removed; with no
  such HASH the CODE is the CONTENT.
R3 Directives. A line with an empty INDENT whose CODE's first token (up to the first SP, or all of it) is a NAME, or a NAME
  followed by one colon, where NAME is an ASCII capital then any of ASCII capitals, digits and hyphens, and NAME is not
  CITED, starts a new directive of that NAME (the colon is dropped). Every other line is a continuation of the latest
  directive, and is dropped when no directive has started yet. Unknown NAMEs are directives too.
R4 Attributes. In the text after a directive's NAME token, and in a continuation's CODE read with one SP put before it, an
  attribute begins at each CITED, EARNED or ANCHOR that is preceded by SP, followed by SP or the end, and not inside a quoted
  span. Everything from the first attribute on is dropped and SP is trimmed from both ends: that is the directive's ARGS,
  or the continuation's BODY. A continuation whose BODY is empty contributes nothing.
R5 No directive named CARRIED -> none. CARRIED-DATA, an indented CARRIED and a CARRIED glued to its name by a non-SP
  character are not CARRIED directives.
R6 A directive named CANONICAL-SHA256, SHAPE or AUTHORITY anywhere in the text (R3: column 0, its colon form included)
  -> throws. Two or more CARRIED directives -> throws. The one CARRIED's ARGS must read NAME SP+ FROM SP+ SOURCE to their end, where
  NAME is an ASCII letter or underscore then any of ASCII letters, digits and underscores, FROM is exactly those four
  capitals, and SOURCE is one or more characters, none of them U+2028 or U+2029. Anything else (an empty ARGS, a binding
  with =, two words before FROM, a non-ASCII name, the head split onto a continuation) -> throws. name = NAME; from = SOURCE
  as written (the SP run after FROM is not part of it; other spaces such as a no-break space are kept).
R7 Sub-fields. Each BODY of the CARRIED's continuations, in order, that starts with CANONICAL-SHA256, SHAPE or AUTHORITY
  (case exact) where the keyword is NOT followed by an ASCII capital, digit or hyphen is a KEYWORD LINE; every other BODY is
  prose and is ignored (a lowercase keyword, SHAPES, SHAPE-X, CANONICAL-SHA2560). A keyword line must be exactly the keyword,
  or the keyword then SP+ then a VALUE of any characters (U+2028 included); VALUE is the text after the SP run (the empty text
  for the bare keyword). Any other keyword line -> throws (a colon, a no-break space or a brace glued to the keyword, a
  lowercase letter or underscore after it). Lines after the next directive (NOTE, ORACLE, an unknown NAME) are not the
  CARRIED's continuations.
R8 CANONICAL-SHA256: a second one -> throws; a VALUE that is not exactly 64 characters of 0-9 a-f (lowercase) -> throws.
  sha = VALUE; no such sub-field -> none.
R9 SHAPE: a second one -> throws; an empty VALUE -> throws (only SP counts as empty: a SHAPE of one no-break space reads).
  shape = VALUE as written; no such sub-field -> none.
R10 AUTHORITY: the VALUE must read, to its end: { SP* expr: SP* STRING SP* , SP* equals: SP* E SP* } — the literals exact
  and in this order, no other field. STRING is a double quote, then any run of (a backslash and any one character, U+2028
  included) or (a character other than a double quote and a backslash), then a double quote. E is everything between the SP run after
  equals: and the SP run before the final closing brace, so it may itself hold braces. Not that form -> throws.
R11 expr = STRING decoded as one JSON string and equals = E parsed as one JSON text (PINNED grammar JSON-VALUE); either not
  JSON -> throws (an E of only SP, an extra field, single quotes, a leading-zero numeral, a raw control character inside the
  string, a no-break space between JSON tokens). Every numeral in E outside its strings is tested against its nearest double
  X: X infinite, or the magnitude of X above 2^53, or X zero while a digit 1-9 appears before the numeral's exponent part (an
  underflow), or an integer-form numeral (an optional minus and digits only) not equal to X exactly -> throws (9007199254740993,
  9007199254740994, 1e16, 1e-400 throw; 9007199254740993.0 reads as 2^53, 0e-400 reads). An expr with no character outside
  the PINNED charset INVISIBLE -> throws. The assertion
  (expr, equals) is appended.
R12 JSON values: a numeral denotes the double nearest its decimal value (ties to even); 1.0 and 1E0 are the number 1; a
  zero spelled with a minus is negative zero. Strings decode the escapes of RFC 8259 section 7 (a surrogate pair of u
  escapes joins into one character). An object is a map; a key repeated in one object keeps its last value. So equals is
  none, bool, f64, text, a list or a map of those.
INVARIANT the result depends only on the CARRIED directive's own line and its continuations, and on whether a directive named
  CANONICAL-SHA256, SHAPE or AUTHORITY exists; any throw leaves no partial result.
OUT-OF-DOMAIN outside ENVELOPE: a u escape of a lone surrogate yields text that is not well-formed (an expr of one reads). Not
  graded.
PINNED charset WS = U+0009 U+000A U+000B U+000C U+000D U+0020 U+00A0 U+1680 U+2000 U+2001 U+2002 U+2003 U+2004 U+2005 U+2006 U+2007 U+2008 U+2009 U+200A U+2028 U+2029 U+202F U+205F U+3000 U+FEFF CITED FIDELITY package — U+0085 and U+180E are NOT in it
PINNED charset CF = U+00AD U+0600-U+0605 U+061C U+06DD U+070F U+0890 U+0891 U+08E2 U+180E U+200B-U+200F U+202A-U+202E U+2060-U+2064 U+2066-U+206F U+FEFF U+FFF9-U+FFFB U+110BD U+110CD U+13430-U+1343F U+1BCA0-U+1BCA3 U+1D173-U+1D17A U+E0001 U+E0020-U+E007F CITED FIDELITY package — General_Category Cf as of Unicode 17.0
PINNED charset INVISIBLE = WS together with CF CITED R11 — the expr emptiness test; U+0085 is visible
PINNED grammar QUOTED-SPAN = scanning left to right, a double quote opens a span when it is the first character, or follows one of space tab ( [ { , = : ; | < > ! & - ~ (not + * ?) or directly follows the closing quote of the previous span; the span closes at the next double quote not taken by an escape, where a backslash takes itself and the next code point; with no closing quote the scan stops and no further span exists; a position is inside when strictly between the opening and closing quotes CITED FIDELITY package — the parser's one literal-protection rule (R2 R4)
PINNED grammar JSON-VALUE = RFC 8259 JSON-text: optional insignificant whitespace (U+0020 U+0009 U+000A U+000D only), one value, optional insignificant whitespace, nothing else CITED FIDELITY package — the reader's JSON parse
ORACLE value -> oracles/carried_declared.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 72cbe3271e1a1ffe36b63c828a86a3fbe9cbfeb8401351a5e74a3564aba72c6e
SCOPE IN: R1–R12 over ENVELOPE, throws included (the message is not graded). OUT: the parser's errors and warnings, what the
  harness later does with the record (hash check, assertion evaluation), the language files' CARRIED bindings.
PROVENANCE rederive@6199e2cc8b75 (MIT), cli/sir/v05.mjs export function carriedDeclared(primaryText) and cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs
  parse / sirLines / splitTrailingComment / splitAttributes / quotedSpans, read from the pinned authority
  selfhost/.authority/6199e2cc8b75/; driven by selfhost/seams/carried_declared.mjs.
